From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: glacier <hyouga.dev@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 01:43:55 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] Store entity bounding boxes as six doubles

Entity.boundingBox was an AxisAlignedBB field, so it outlived the tick
and could never be scalar-replaced: every change to any axis allocated a
fresh 64-byte box, several times per entity per tick, plus one more for
each world query built with grow/shrink/offset/addCoord.

Replace the field with boundingBoxMinX..boundingBoxMaxZ and drop
getBoundingBox() entirely rather than leaving a bridge (a bridge would
have to allocate a box on every read, which is worse than the status
quo). setEntityBoundingBox now takes six doubles and keeps the
CraftBukkit size clamping.

Consumers gained scalar overloads (World entity/material/collision
queries, Chunk entity queries, WorldBorder.contains, Explosion block
density, EntityEnderDragon.destroyBlocksInAABB), and the box-producing
operations are expanded at the call site, reproducing the AxisAlignedBB
constructor's Math.min/max normalization where a negative delta can
invert an axis (grow/shrink); offset/addCoord/union preserve ordering
and need none.

Also converts org.spigotmc.ActivationRange, which rebuilt four boxes for
every player every tick.

Entity.move now carries every box - including the step-up branch's four
simultaneous candidates - in local doubles, which cost nothing.

getCubes/getCollisionBoxes keep an AxisAlignedBB internally for now: the
block collision API still takes one. That is the next step.

Verified: full test suite passes (AxisAlignedBBHelperTest pins the
scalar math against the original class), server boots on JDK 25 and
ticks 30s with no exceptions or movement warnings.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>

